 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per pixel, where $N$ is the number of samples along the ray. The operations per sample are constant time (dot product, clamping, linear interpolation).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per sample.
-*   **Strengths:** Enhances boundary visibility without requiring complex multi-dimensional transfer functions or additional data structures [S1]. It is computationally inexpensive and suitable for real-time rendering [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the availability of accurate normal vectors, which may require additional computation (e.g., gradient estimation) if not pre-computed. The "bubble-like" effect may not be suitable for all data types or visualization goals. The source notes do not provide measured performance benchmarks [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample. The calculation involves a dot product, a few arithmetic operations, and clamps. It adds negligible overhead to the ray casting loop [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No additional storage is required beyond the parameters [S2].
+*   **Performance:** The method is efficient and can be implemented in real-time using Programmable Transfer Functions (PTFs) on the GPU without increasing storage requirements compared to multi-dimensional transfer functions [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Enhances depth perception, reveals boundaries of isosurfaces, and helps distinguish flat features from volumetric structures [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate gradient estimation. The "bubble" effect may not be suitable for all data types (e.g., if dark edges are preferred) [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-Silhouette shading was introduced in the context of visualizing WiFi signals using Programmable Transfer Functions (PTF) [S1]. The method is described as being based on the idea that silhouettes of isosurfaces have normals perpendicular to the viewing angle, while central parts have normals aligned with the view direction [S1]. The effect is attributed to Demir et al. [30] in the source [S1]. The technique allows analysts to discern features in volumes more clearly, particularly flat regions that might otherwise be indistinguishable [S1].
+The technique is inspired by astrophysical limb darkening, where the center of a star appears brighter than the edges (limb) due to the depth of observation into the atmosphere [S1]. In volume visualization, this concept is adapted to enhance 3D perception. The specific "bubble-like" shading algorithm using Programmable Transfer Functions was introduced to address the difficulty analysts face in discerning features in flat volume renderings [S2]. It references Demir et al. [30] for the underlying effect description [S2].
 
